{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_2618ecad05324c2aaa72cf2d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns yang.clojure\n2\t  \"Yang Clojure compiler - Compiles Clojure code into Yin VM Universal AST.\n3\t\n4\t  This is the Clojure-specific compiler in the Yang compiler collection.\n5\t  It transforms Clojure s-expressions into the Universal AST format that\n6\t  the Yin VM can execute. It handles:\n7\t  - Literals (numbers, strings, booleans, nil, collections)\n8\t  - Variables (symbols)\n9\t  - Lambda expressions (fn)\n10\t  - Function application (including stream operations via module system)\n11\t  - Conditionals (if)\n12\t  - Let bindings (let)\n13\t  - Top-level definitions via explicit yin/def store effects\n14\t  - Macro definitions (defmacro)\n15\t\n16\t  Macro support (docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md §10.2):\n17\t  - defmacro is a special form that lowers to ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...))\n18\t    syntax. The lambda node carries `:macro? true`, a frontend-internal fact\n19\t    the encoder (`yin.vm.encoder/program-batch`) turns into a declaration row;\n20\t    the canonical lambda row has no macro slot.\n21\t  - A macro call is an ordinary application. The frontend does no macro\n22\t    lookup: the row-native expander recognizes the call.\n23\t  - compile-program threads a compile-time macro-env naming the defmacros\n24\t    seen so far, only so the native defn lowering yields to a user defn macro.\n25\t\n26\t  Stream operations (stream/make, stream/put, stream/take, >!, <!) are compiled\n27\t  as regular function applications and resolved through the module system at runtime.\n28\t\n29\t  The compiler is written in .cljc format to run on both JVM and Node.js.\"\n30\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [compile])\n31\t  (:require #?@(:jolt []\n32\t                :cljd []\n33\t                :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\n34\t                      [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]]\n35\t                :cljs [[cljs