{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119001726b22a624984254faf","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good — I now understand the flow. The emitter is `compile-constructed-collection`, called from `compile-form*` when `literal?` is true but `constant-form?` is false.\n\nKey facts for the fix:\n- Map branch: `(into [] (interleave (keys form) (vals form)))` — host traversal.\n- Set branch: `(vec form)`.\n- Vector: `(vec form)` unchanged.\n\nImplementation plan for the emitter per RULING 1:\n\n```clojure\n(defn- semantic-root\n  \"The canonical semantic-bytecode root address of compiled `ast`: the\n   content identity of its projected executable content, used as the\n   canonical sort key of constructed-collection operands (constructed\n   collections v1).\"\n  [ast]\n  (:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))\n```\n\nHmm wait — `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` on a child node. The child may be any node type. It computes rows for the subtree. Root is a segment key. OK.\n\nNew emitter:\n\n```clojure\n(defn- compile-constructed-collection\n  \"Lower a collection literal with dynamic elements to constructor calls over\n   existing portable primitives, so unquoted vectors/maps/sets evaluate their\n   elements like Clojure. Constant collections stay :literal nodes.\n\n   [e1 e2 ...]  => (conj [] e1 e2 ...)\n   #{e1 e2 ...} => (conj #{} e1 e2 ...)\n   {k1 v1 ...}  => (assoc {} k1 v1 k2 v2 ...)\n\n   Constructed collections v1: a host map or set has no portable iteration\n   order, so the emitted operand vector is the compiled-content order —\n   pairs/elements sorted ascending by jing/canonical-compare over the\n   children's semantic-bytecode root addresses ([key-root value-root] for a\n   map pair, the element root for a set), after the seed operand. This is\n   compile-time inspection of code, never evaluation; a vector keeps its\n   written order. Equal sort keys are equal executable content; every\n   operand occurrence is preserved.\"\n  [form tail? env macro-env]\n  (let [ctor (cond\n               (vector? form) ['conj []]\n               (set? form) ['conj #{}]\n               :else ['assoc {}])\n        [op seed] ctor\n        compile-one #(compile-form % false env macro-env)\n        operands\n        (cond\n          (vector? form) (mapv compile-one form)\n          (map? form)\n          (->> (map (fn [k v]\n                      (let [ck (compile-one k)\n                            cv (compile-one v)]\n                        {:sort-key [(semantic-root ck) (semantic-root cv)]\n                         :asts [ck cv]}))\n                    (keys form) (vals form))\n               (sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare)\n               (mapcat :asts)\n               vec)\n          :else\n          (->> (map (fn [e]\n                      (let [ce (compile-one e)]\n                        {:sort-key [(semantic-root ce)] :asts [ce]}))\n                    form)\n               (sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare)\n               (mapcat :asts)\n               vec))\n        call {:type :application,\n              :operator {:type :variable, :name op},\n              :operands (into [{:type :literal, :value seed}] operands)}]\n    (cond-> call tail? (assoc :tail? true))))\n```\n\nWait — `sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare` — sort-by calls `(comp (keyfn x) (keyfn y))`, i.e., `jing/canonical-compare` applied to the two sort-key vectors. Good: comparator over the two-element vector of address values. \"The concrete key is the vector of two address values, not its printer rendering and not the hash of that vector.\" ✓.\n\nBut careful: `sort-by` with a Java comparator on CLJS — `sort-by` in ClojureScript requires the comparator to return a number or boolean; `canonical-compare` returns -1/0/1 numbers. Fine.\n\nCLJD (ClojureDart): `sort-by` exists; comparator must be a proper 3-way comparator. Returns -1/0/1. Fine.\n\nJolt: `(:jolt [])` in the ns means on the jolt lane, yang.clojure doesn't require treader etc. Does the jolt host support sort-by/jing? The J2 lane runs `yin.vm` etc. `dao.jing` should be available on jolt. Let me check what `dao.jing` supports on the :jolt reader conditional... The jolt lane compiles ClojureScript-compatible code to Jolt? J2 lane runs test namespaces under Jolt (a guest language runtime). Since `dao.jing` is used by `yin.vm` heavily, it must work on jolt.\n\nNow — one subtlety: `semantic-root` calls `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` per child. For nested collections, children were already compiled; their subtrees get projected repeatedly (each parent re-projects its children). That's compile-time cost, acceptable (the ruling acknowledges \"additional compile-time content projection/sorting costs\").\n\nAnother subtlety: `ast->semantic-bytecode` may THROW on unknown node types. Are there node types among compiled children not in `semantic-bytecode-grammar`? Children are results of `compile-form`, so they're valid UAST nodes... but are ALL UAST node types in the grammar? E.g. `:literal`, `:variable`, `:application`, `:lambda`, `:if`, `:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:stream/make`, `:vm/current-continuation`... Let me check `semantic-bytecode-grammar` to make sure every node type compile-form can produce is projectable. Actually — `validate-ast-call-sites!` already projects whole produced trees via `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`, so any tree that compile-form produces must already be projectable (C1 validation runs on production trees). Children of a collection constructor are subtrees of the final tree, so projectable. \n\nBut wait — there's an ordering nuance: `annotate-call-sites` runs at the END of the top-level `compile-form` — so during nested compilation (inside `compile-form*`), child ASTs don't yet carry `:yang/call-site` keys. `ast->semantic-bytecode` drops `:yang/*` keys anyway. Fine.\n\nAnother nuance: `compile-form` (not `compile-form-1`) — I must call the same entry the old code called: old code called `(compile-form % false env macro-env)`. The recursive call from `compile-constructed-collection` goes through `compile-form` which, inside a compilation (with `*call-site-finalizing*` bound), just calls `compile-form-1`. Same as before. Keep using `compile-form`.\n\nNow — CRITICAL correctness question: `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` uses an atom for dedup and collision checks. Calling it per child is safe (pure).\n\nAnother question: does `:root` from `ast->semantic-bytecode` return a stable keyword across hosts? Yes — that's the whole point (content-addressed rows).\n\nNow, metadata: \"Keep the original AST, including its attached frontend/source metadata, with its paired record.\" — I keep `ck`/`cv` ASTs as-is. ✓ \"The projected rows used to obtain a sort key are temporary compiler data, not published roots...\" — I don't publish them. ✓\n\nNow the manifest (RULING 4):\n\n```clojure\n(defn clojure-manifest\n  \"...\"\n  []\n  (assoc (manifest :yang/clojure :clojure)\n         :yang.frontend/revision \"c1-2\"\n         :yang.frontend/lowering-profile :yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1\n         :yang.frontend/support-profile :yang.clojure/c1))\n```\n\nWait — ruling: 'advance the Clojure frontend manifest revision from the inspected `\"c1-1\"` to `\"c1-2\"` and declare its lowering profile as `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`'. The default lowering-profile is `:segment/legacy`; c1-1 overrode support-profile only. Now we also override lowering-profile. Keep support-profile as `:yang.clojure/c1` (\"Keep the C1 marker value 1 and the existing C1 support claim\"). Also update the docstring.\n\nNow — before making changes, run the baseline. Let me set up the environment:\n\n- `export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/share/mise/shims:$PATH\"`\n- `bb gen:antlr`\n- `clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test` (baseline JVM: 45 tests green)\n\nThen write tests RED.\n\nLet me now check what test namespaces exist for manifest expectations: `test/yin/repl/frontend_catalog_test.cljc` mentions \"c1-1\" presumably. Let me grep for \"c1-1\" across the repo to find all places that pin the revision.","signature":"2c259085f3834f5bb1c44cc2"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"4bb17adc-e2dd-4726-b5c8-189cb5865efa","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:17:52.265Z","thinking_duration_ms":24544,"thinking_display":"updates"}
