{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119002213ad71fd1f23714d3c","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`assoc` and `conj` are pure VM primitives. `identity`, `inc`, `dec`, `count`, `data/count` — `data/count` comes from the data module (`data/register-data-module`). `identity`/`inc`/`dec` are NOT in vm/primitives. So for runtime tests, use only primitives in the registry: `+ - * / = < > not first rest conj assoc get vec` + data module fns (data/count, data/content=, ...). Or pass an :env with host fns? `compile-and-run` accepts env: `(compile-and-run form {'x 100})` — env maps variables to values. Variables resolve from env — I could bind `inc`? No — variable lookup happens... Let me check how the walker resolves `{:type :variable :name 'inc}` — via env or primitives. If env supplies it, `(compile-and-run '((fn [x] x) 1))`... simpler to use primitives directly: use `(+ x 1)` and `(- x 1)` as dynamic key expressions (both applications, different content). For colliding keys: `{(+ 1 1) :a (+ 2 0) :b}` — both evaluate to 2, different code. Good, and both key exprs are applications of `+`.\n\nFor the trace/effect-order tests (family 5): \"Record an observable trace using the existing test harness's explicit effects/streams: key-before-value, canonical pair/element order, no duplication, and no later event after an operand failure/continuation transfer.\"\n\nObservable trace — how do tests record evaluation order? Options: use effectful primitives... `vm/primitives` has no effectful user-callable trace primitive except require/try-require. But `clojure_test` base-vm-opts registers stream module — `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo ...)`. Hmm. Alternatively bind host functions in the env: does create-vm's :env supply variable bindings as functions? In `compile-and-run`, env maps var names to values. If I bind `probe` to a host function that appends to an atom, then `(probe 1)` compiles as application of variable `probe` → looks up env → host function → calls it. Does the walker/semantic/stack/register VM support calling host functions from env? The values module presumably wraps host fns. Let me check how env values that are functions get applied — search for tests calling env-supplied functions.","signature":"c1eb1a8dcac14dd2bed1e63c"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"09bd2876-81f8-4e67-affe-ac211a92f4e2","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:22:25.605Z","thinking_duration_ms":8266,"thinking_display":"updates"}
