ap/hash-map representations\" — the test constructs maps as DATA (host values) passed to yang/compile. A host map literal `{k1 v1 k2 v2}` on JVM with ≤8 entries is an array-map (insertion-ordered); `(apply hash-map ...)` gives a hash-map. Since compile-constructed-collection receives the map VALUE, different host representations with equal content must produce identical compiled trees post-fix (they will: sorted by content).\n\nPermutations: for sizes 0–4 enumerate all permutations of the entry vector; for 8, 9, 16, 32 use recorded forward/reverse/rotated/fixed-shuffle permutations. Record as explicit data (no host rand). Deterministic shuffle: a recorded fixed permutation vector, or an explicitly specified deterministic generator with recorded seeds — e.g. a simple LCG implemented in the test with fixed seed. The ruling allows \"an explicitly specified deterministic generator with recorded seeds\". I'll implement a tiny explicit permutation generator (LCG) with fixed seed constants, plus explicit forward/reverse/rotations.\n\nEntries must include: constant keys/dynamic values, dynamic keys, heterogeneous scalar and quoted collection keys, nested maps/sets/vectors, floats, generated destructuring names, duplicate subtrees, reader/non-reader metadata. And \"At least one dynamic expression must reach the emitter in each nonconstant case.\"\n\nHmm — \"generated destructuring names\" — e.g. symbols named `dsg__av`? No — generated names appear when compiling fn destructuring; in a collection, a symbol value like 'dsg__av would be rejected by check-program-symbols (reserved prefix) at compile-program level, but plain `yang/compile` of a form doesn't run that pre-pass. But destructuring-generated names inside a collection: compile `(fn [[a b] c] {a [b]})` — the map contains variable 'a, 'b — locals. To have \"generated destructuring names\" reach the emitter as compiled children, the collection must appear inside a destructuring context, e.g. `((fn [[a b]] {a b, b a}) [1 2