nged). I'll assert root + operand order + C1 digest invariance.\n\nWait — actually careful: with `with-meta` on a form, `compile-form-1` merges meta onto the AST node (line 2067-2069: `(vary-meta ast merge ...)`). Node metadata (reader positions) is dropped by projection. But NON-position metadata like `:note`? `compile-form-1` merges the whole meta map onto the AST node's metadata (not node keys). Projection: node METADATA (Clojure meta) — `strip-reader-positions` only for :data/:key payloads... What does `ast->semantic-bytecode` do with node-level meta? `convert` looks only at `:type` and grammar slots — node meta is ignored entirely (it's `get node field`, meta not a key). So node meta doesn't affect rows. But the ENCODER (`encoder/project`) writes frontend-metadata entries for reader metadata — as a separate table. So roots/C1 markers unchanged. \n\n\"Preserve semantic metadata in literal payloads\" (family 4) — `^:replay`-style metadata inside literal values? The r2 cohort uses `^:replay` on a form... `strip-reader-positions` keeps non-position metadata in data payloads (\"data/key payloads lose reader positions at every depth and keep all other metadata\"). So a quoted literal `'^{:semantic true} x` as a map key: the metadata is part of the data payload, affects content. Test: a quoted form with metadata as a key vs the same without — different roots (metadata is semantic), but both sort consistently. And identical metadata → same root across hosts. I'll include quoted-collection keys with metadata.\n\nFamily 6 (entry points): compile the same collection through `compile-form`, `compile`, `compile-program` (both modes: native and `true yang/unit-opts`), and `lower-unit`; assert operand vector obeys canonical order in each; macro expansion chain: use `expand-like-the-repl`-style expansion — hmm, that helper is in the callsite test. For my new namespace, I'll write a small local version or reuse via requiring... The callsite test ns is a test namespace; requiring one test ns from another is unusual but legal. Better: local minimal helper using `macro/expand` — let me look at what `expand-like-the-repl` does.\n\nAlso family 6: \"Keep the real production macro/twin-call fixture and the complete expansion-record chain in the equality gate\" — that's in the callsite cohort (macro member) — preserved by keeping the cohort. My new namespace adds: compiler-generated arity maps (argument-vector mode) — the arity-map order check per family 1 covers that.\n\n\"Prove that pre-expansion collection normalization does not fabricate or erase expansion origins\": the macro member in the cohort pins the expansion log — kept. In my namespace: expand a macro whose expansion introduces a constructed map; assert the expansion records/origins are intact (status :ok, twin origins present). Let me look at the macro test helpers.\n\nFamily 7 (legacy load): \"Load a retained pre-fix artifact by its old address and execute it without recompilation; retain its old C1/provenance if present.\" The pre-fix artifact: `test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-jvm.cbor` — how is it loaded? Let me check how the .edn captures are read in tests (cc4-replay-snapshots.edn is read where?) — actually `replay-expectations` is literal in the test; the resource EDN files are... let me check if any test reads them. grep.","signature":"c57eb092250b4a19a1cd2a17"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"9df350a1-0f47-4dd9-be6a-798097d5eaf3","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:23:14.643Z","thinking_duration_ms":44135,"thinking_display":"updates"}
