{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026111900232317510079531f4c18","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The divergence EDN holds complete actual Dart trees with rows keyed by segment address. This is immutable evidence. \n\nFor the legacy-load test, the cleanest pre-fix artifact is this: a pre-fix compiled TREE (rows + root) I can load into the row lane and EXECUTE without recompilation. But executing the argument-vector tree needs the linked runtime modules (clj module etc.) — heavy. Better: capture a PRE-FIX compiled tree of a simple executable program containing a constructed map (native mode), store it as a NEW fixture (recorded pre-fix, e.g. from the current HEAD compiler — I can generate it right now before the fix, since the worktree is still at pre-fix state!). Then the family-7 test loads that fixture by old address and executes it (native walker/semantic lane), asserting the OLD operand order executes with old behavior (e.g. colliding-keys case: old order determines which value wins), and its root stays the old root when re-derived... \"retain its old C1/provenance if present\" — assert the loaded tree's root equals the recorded old root.\n\nSo: TODAY (pre-fix), I capture fixture data: a small native program with a constructed map (including a runtime-colliding-key case whose winner differs between old JVM order and new canonical order — that's a great artifact: executing the legacy artifact post-fix must still yield the OLD result, proving old artifacts keep old behavior).\n\nLet me design that carefully:\n- Program: `(fn [x] {(+ x 1) :a (- x 1) :b})` applied to 1 → keys both 2. Old JVM order: host map {(+ x 1) :a, (- x 1) :b} — small map → JVM array-map? The form is a map literal with 2 entries built by the READER of the test (host Clojure) — array-map, insertion order preserved: (+ x 1) first, (- x 1) second → old order: (+ x 1) :a then (- x 1) :b → later wins → key 2 = :b.\n- New canonical order: sort by [key-root value-root]. key roots: applications (+ x 1) vs (- x 1) — need actual bytes: symbols `+` vs `-`. CBOR text for \"+\": 0x63 0x2B...; \"-\": 0x63 0x2D. \"+\" (0x2B) < \"-\" (0x2D). So (+ x 1) root < (- x 1) root? The variable rows differ ('+ vs '-) → bytewise + < -. But wait — row ids are hashed (blake3 of the row body) — the ROOT ID is a hash, so ordering by ID bytes is essentially random relative to symbol names! The comparator orders by canonical bytes of the ADDRESS VALUES (`:segment/blake3-<hex>` keywords — CBOR text of the hex string). So which application sorts first depends on the blake3 hex of their rows — effectively arbitrary but DETERMINISTIC. So I can't hand-predict; the test must derive expected order via the independent builder (project children, compute roots, sort). Good — that's exactly what the ruling mandates (independent expectation builder).\n\nFor the legacy fixture: I record (pre-fix) the compiled tree rows+root AND the executed result (:a or :b per old JVM order). Post-fix test: load fixture rows into VM, execute, assert old result. Also compile the same source fresh, assert new canonical order, and (likely) different root than fixture root, and assert the winner is the canonically-last pair.\n\nAlso for family 7: \"retain the unannotated legacy-load/missing-C1-refusal tests\" — those are the existing `published-instruction-provenance-test`/`unclaimed-batch-test` in the callsite battery — untouched. And \"admitting the same content with two different tokens must still produce different origins and occurrence keys\" — the cohort's negative member + the last assertions of portable-replay-cohort-test — preserved.\n\nNow let me nail down the mechanics of loading + executing rows from a fixture. `tu/queue-rows!` queues `(vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)` row-set onto the session's program medium; the loader for rows... `run-encoder-session` uses linearize/project-rows + semantic/load-vector. But simpler: `queue-rows!` + `run-session` — what loader does the plain observer session use for row batches? In run-session... let me read run-session and how a row set on the medium is consumed.","signature":"dccc9f560bd04bcaa746febc"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"987a4389-dd50-49f4-a0e3-0dd4f76fb161","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:23:42.066Z","thinking_duration_ms":15917,"thinking_display":"updates"}
