{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_89fe1915c96d48b592e7696f","type":"tool_result","content":"925\t                                     (assoc index\n926\t                                            [(get nodes (nth row 3))\n927\t                                             (nth row 2)]\n928\t                                            id)\n929\t                                     index))\n930\t                                 (or (:row-index vm) {})\n931\t                                 rows))))\n932\t\n933\t\n934\t(defn vm-load-rows\n935\t  \"Load one semantic-bytecode row set `{:root id, :rows {id row}}` into the\n936\t   VM: `vm/semantic-bytecode->ast` runs the §7.4 validator first and throws\n937\t   `ex-info` carrying the defect's `:rule` and `:path` (or `:id`), then\n938\t   reconstructs the map AST, and this applies the same execution-field\n939\t   updates as `vm-load-program`. This is §9.1's loader, the successor of\n940\t   the datom decode; the datom loader stays alongside it — both are legal\n941\t   per-composition choices, and a composition wires whichever loader(s) it\n942\t   wants.\n943\t\n944\t   `contract` is required and compared with `vm/ast-contract` first, as\n945\t   for `vm-load-program`; S7.4 validation includes Rule R. The rows join\n946\t   the held rows and the `[node params]` index, as `attach-image` adds\n947\t   them, and every `:lambda` node of the tree carries its row id.\"\n948\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm bc contract]\n949\t  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract contract)\n950\t  (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast bc)\n951\t  (let [held (hold-rows vm (:rows bc))\n952\t        ast (get (:row-nodes held) (:root bc))]\n953\t    (assoc held\n954\t           :program ast\n955\t           :control ast\n956\t           :halted? false\n957\t           :blocked? false\n958\t           :value nil)))\n959\t\n960\t\n961\t(defn attach-image\n962\t  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with the row set `bc`, non-destructively\n963\t   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, act 3): `bc` is admitted as\n964\t   `vm-load-rows` admits it and its rows are added to the held rows by\n965\t   id, the `[node params]` index extended; the current node, environment,\n966\t   continuation, and store are unchanged. Rows are content-addressed, so\n967\t   a row already held is the same row.\"\n968\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm bc contract]\n969\t  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract contract)\n970\t  (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast bc)\n971\t  (hold-rows vm (:rows bc)))\n972\t\n973\t\n974\t(defn attach-rows\n975\t  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with the flat addressed `rows`\n976\t   (`{id [id tag & slots]}`), however many roots they serve: the rows\n977\t   join the held rows by id, each decoded once into `:row-nodes` and\n978\t   every `:lambda` row indexed by `[body-node params]`.  The caller has\n979\t   validated each row (`vm/validate-rows` over each root); nothing here\n980\t   runs a program, and the current node, environment, continuation and\n981\t   store are unchanged.  `contract` is compared with `vm/ast-contract`\n982\t   first.\"\n983\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm rows contract]\n984\t  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract contract)\n985\t  (hold-rows vm rows))\n986\t\n987\t\n988\t(defn clear-code\n989\t  \"`vm` holding no rows at all: the kernel's code layout, emptied before\n990\t   a lower rebuilds it.\"\n991\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm]\n992\t  (assoc vm :rows {} :row-nodes {} :row-index {}))\n993\t\n994\t\n995\t(defn row-node\n996\t  \"The node the walker's row decoder built for held row `id`, or nil when\n997\t   no such row is held. A lower reconstructs a closure's body this way.\n998\t   The decode ran once, when the row was loaded or attached.\"\n999\t  [vm id]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"08d8cb4e-b071-4018-b6dc-8136404dd9da","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:23:51.528Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc","content":"                                     (assoc index\n                                            [(get nodes (nth row 3))\n                                             (nth row 2)]\n                                            id)\n                                     index))\n                                 (or (:row-index vm) {})\n                                 rows))))\n\n\n(defn vm-load-rows\n  \"Load one semantic-bytecode row set `{:root id, :rows {id row}}` into the\n   VM: `vm/semantic-bytecode->ast` runs the §7.4 validator first and throws\n   `ex-info` carrying the defect's `:rule` and `:path` (or `:id`), then\n   reconstructs the map AST, and this applies the same execution-field\n   updates as `vm-load-program`. This is §9.1's loader, the successor of\n   the datom decode; the datom loader stays alongside it — both are legal\n   per-composition choices, and a composition wires whichever loader(s) it\n   wants.\n\n   `contract` is required and compared with `vm/ast-contract` first, as\n   for `vm-load-program`; S7.4 validation includes Rule R. The rows join\n   the held rows and the `[node params]` index, as `attach-image` adds\n   them, and every `:lambda` node of the tree carries its row id.\"\n  [^ASTWalkerVM vm bc contract]\n  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract contract)\n  (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast bc)\n  (let [held (hold-rows vm (:rows bc))\n        ast (get (:row-nodes held) (:root bc))]\n    (assoc held\n           :program ast\n           :control ast\n           :halted? false\n           :blocked? false\n           :value nil)))\n\n\n(defn attach-image\n  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with the row set `bc`, non-destructively\n   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, act 3): `bc` is admitted as\n   `vm-load-rows` admits it and its rows are added to the held rows by\n   id, the `[node params]` index extended; the current node, environment,\n   continuation, and store are unchanged. Rows are content-addressed, so\n   a row already held is the same row.\"\n  [^ASTWalkerVM vm bc contract]\n  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract contract)\n  (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast bc)\n  (hold-rows vm (:rows bc)))\n\n\n(defn attach-rows\n  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with the flat addressed `rows`\n   (`{id [id tag & slots]}`), however many roots they serve: the rows\n   join the held rows by id, each decoded once into `:row-nodes` and\n   every `:lambda` row indexed by `[body-node params]`.  The caller has\n   validated each row (`vm/validate-rows` over each root); nothing here\n   runs a program, and the current node, environment, continuation and\n   store are unchanged.  `contract` is compared with `vm/ast-contract`\n   first.\"\n  [^ASTWalkerVM vm rows contract]\n  (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract contract)\n  (hold-rows vm rows))\n\n\n(defn clear-code\n  \"`vm` holding no rows at all: the kernel's code layout, emptied before\n   a lower rebuilds it.\"\n  [^ASTWalkerVM vm]\n  (assoc vm :rows {} :row-nodes {} :row-index {}))\n\n\n(defn row-node\n  \"The node the walker's row decoder built for held row `id`, or nil when\n   no such row is held. A lower reconstructs a closure's body this way.\n   The decode ran once, when the row was loaded or attached.\"\n  [vm id]","numLines":75,"startLine":925,"totalLines":1275}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"call_89fe1915c96d48b592e7696f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
