{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026111900251813ae3f6eae7e4284","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, `grep -c` shows test_utils.cljc is MISSING from the output — the first line lists ast_walker but not test_utils... Actually the output starts with `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:0` — test_utils.cljc is absent, meaning grep found no match and returned... no, -c prints 0 for each file. The output shows 5 files; test_utils is missing — maybe grep errored on it? Or the output got truncated. Anyway `yang.core.call-test` has no :cljd gates and uses test-utils + runners, and `test/yang/python/antlr/*_test.cljc` too — those run on Dart (python worktree lanes ran int_ops etc. on multiple hosts per memory). So test-utils IS portable across hosts including Dart.\n\nWhat about Jolt? yang.core.call-test is NOT in the J2 included list — so it's never run on Jolt. My new namespace WILL be on Jolt (I add it to the manifest). Do the runners (ast-walker, debruijn stack/register, test-utils' dao.stream.ringbuffer) work on Jolt? yin.vm.ast-walker-test IS in included (line 275). yin.vm.debruijn.stack-test, debruijn-register-code-test etc. are included. dao.stream.ringbuffer-test included (line 254). test-utils itself is a helper ns, not a test — fine as long as it loads. dao.stream.memory-log — dao.stream.journal uses...? memory-log-test isn't in the list but wait — line 250: \"dao.stream.memory-log-test\" YES included.\n\nOK so test-utils should load on Jolt. But — risk: J2 lane runs JOLT via its own classpath/source root. The safest approach for the Jolt lane: keep my namespace's heavy requires minimal, and structure execution tests so they can run on all hosts. But the ruling demands \"Run portable fixtures through walker, semantic, stack, and register execution where those existing routes support them, on the four host lanes.\"\n\nThe four runners approach from call_test uses: yin.vm.test-utils (tu/create-vm, tu/secret, tu/make-stream), yin.vm.ast-walker (vm-load? actually vm/eval), yin.vm.semantic, derive, debruijn stack/register. All included-adjacent on Jolt. I'll go with it.\n\nOne concern: `vm/eval` — call_test's `:ast-walker` runner: `(vm/eval (tu/create-vm opts) ast)`. vm/eval exists in yin.vm. OK.\n\nAlso `tu/make-stream` needs ringbuffer — on Jolt should work (ringbuffer-test is included).\n\nNow — the J2/Jolt lane: my namespace will be added to `included` and `bare`. Note: the J2 lane currently REFUSES at manifest because of the four unrelated unlisted namespaces. So my Jolt gate will ALSO refuse — recorded honestly. Hmm wait — will it? The r2 report says J2 refuses naming `yin.repl.php-parser-test`, `yin.vm.code-entry-test`, `yin.vm.machine-data-test`, `yin.vm.provenance-test`. Those still exist unlisted → my Jolt run will exit 1 at manifest before running anything. The ruling: \"J2's four-namespace manifest refusal MUST be resolved by its owner before four-host acceptance; zero Jolt children is not a pass.\" So I record the refusal. But — can I still get SOME Jolt evidence? The refusal happens in check-manifest! before lane work. No bypass permitted (\"The optional namespace selector cannot bypass this check\"). So Jolt = honest refusal record.\n\nNow — the callsite test on Jolt DID run in cc4 r1? No — r2's table says Jolt exit 1 at manifest, 0 children. So \"four-host\" for this round = JVM, Node, Dart + Jolt-refusal-record. The ruling's test family 1 says \"Assert the constructor row has the same ordered child-address vector on JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt\" — with Jolt blocked by the owner dependency, I record that honestly.\n\nNow, let me also confirm how `vm/eval` works and the semantic lane loading — I'll copy call_test's runners verbatim style.\n\nNext: understand `annotate-call-sites` (does it walk operand vectors in order? paths by index — order changes paths of later operands only).\n\nOne IMPORTANT question for my implementation: sort STABILITY across hosts for ties (equal [k-root v-root]). sort-by in Clojure is stable (TimSort). ClojureScript's sort: `sort` calls `garray/stableSort` — stable. ClojureDart: sort uses... let me check ClojureDart's sort implementation — it's in clojure.core dart implementation. ClojureDart's `sort` uses `List.sort` with a comparison — Dart's List.sort is NOT guaranteed stable! Actually Dart's sort implementation (dual-pivot quicksort-ish) is unstable. Hmm — ClojureDart might implement stable sort itself. Let me check the vendored clojure.core cljd source... This matters only for ties: identical [k-root v-root] pairs — identical compiled content. As analyzed, tied pairs' permutation doesn't change canonical rows or sorted key vectors; the C1 occurrence-key vector is sorted canonically before digesting; per-position marker entries [origin root path :yang/call-site 1] — tied pairs swap between positions p and p' — the SET of marked entries is unchanged (same roots at same paths? wait — path p would then hold the OTHER AST object, but both objects have the SAME projected row id (same content) — so the occurrence [root path] entries are identical as data. The frontend metadata from reader positions could differ (different :line on each) — but position rows are [origin root path k v] with different values — those WOULD swap between hosts... but they're not part of C1/keys digests (only :yang/call-site filtered). BUT my family-2 snapshot includes full C1/keys + provenance — call-provenance is derived from the semantic vector — tied operands produce identical instructions at both positions, so instruction provenance identical. However `expansion-log`/full frontend-metadata comparisons aren't in my permutation snapshot. To be safe, my family-2 corpus should include ties but ALSO assert digests that don't depend on per-node metadata. Permutation entries share the same source forms anyway (same metadata on same forms across permutations... actually permutations permute the ORDER of the same form objects; with-meta forms keep their metadata — the metadata side table is keyed by path; a permuted map puts form A at path 3 vs path 5 — the [origin root path :line ...] rows DIFFER between permutations! So full frontend-metadata comparison across permutations is NOT invariant (positions differ) — that's why the cohort compares only C1 markers + provenance. I'll compare root + C1/keys digests + provenance digest (metadata-insensitive except call-site entries which are path-keyed but content-identical). \n\nHold on — are C1 marker entries path-dependent across permutations? Marker entry = [origin root path :yang/call-site 1]. Permutation P1: pair X at path [3 1], pair Y at [3 3]. P2: Y at [3 1], X at [3 3]. The SET of entries: {[o r [3 1] ...1], [o r [3 3] ...1]} — SAME set (values identical, roots identical since tree root is same and the occurrence content is the same). Yes — the sorted C1 relation is permutation-invariant. \n\nAnd the KEYS digest (marked rows, [origin root path]) — same set. Provenance: instruction call-provenance per application occurrence — tied/permuted pairs yield same instruction content at swapped positions; call-provenance records provenance per instruction... includes paths? If provenance keys include occurrence paths, the multiset is still identical (same set of [path, app-content] pairs across permutations? P1: [3 1]→X-content; P2: [3 1]→Y-content. If X≠Y (non-tied), the path→content mapping DIFFERS! Wait no — non-tied pairs get canonically SORTED, so after the fix both permutations emit the SAME operand order → identical path→content mapping. Only TIED pairs can differ, and tied = identical content → identical mapping. Phew. So post-fix, permutations yield byte-identical everything except reader-position metadata rows. Good.\n\nThe call-provenance: derived from linearize with frontend metadata? In replay-snapshot: `(linearize/project-rows tree nil rows)` where rows = frontend metadata — provenance relates instruction addresses to occurrence paths... Fine — post-fix identical.\n\nNow the critical question about permutation INVARIANCE of what I compare: I'll compare `snapshot` = root + keys-digest + c1-digest + instruction-provenance-digest (cohort model) — all permutation-invariant post-fix. RED pre-fix: permutations of insertion order → different host-map iteration → different operand order → different root. But CAREFUL: on the JVM with ≤8 entries, array-map preserves insertion; for permutations enumerated for sizes 0-4 (≤24 entries each)... sizes 0-4 = up to 4! = 24 permutations — array-map (≤8 entries = insertion-ordered). So two different insertion orders give different iteration orders → different operand vectors → different roots (unless the sort... there's no sort pre-fix). So pre-fix RED: at least two permutations of a ≥2-entry dynamic map differ in root. ✓. Post-fix all equal. ✓.\n\nRepresentation crossing: array-map vs hash-map for sizes 8/9/16/32 — hash-map iteration order differs from insertion; post-fix invariant regardless. Pre-fix RED via hash-map vs array-map same insertion → likely different order (JVM hashing)... For the RED proof I just need SOME inequality pre-fix; the 0-4 permutation case suffices.\n\nNow — an important question: how do I CONSTRUCT these maps as forms to compile? `(yang/compile form)` where form is a host map value. For \"insertion permutations\", build `(apply array-map k1 v1 k2 v2 ...)` in each permutation order; for hash-map representation `(apply hash-map ...)` (unordered arg order still defines insertion for hashing). Note hash-map with >8 entries on JVM; array-map beyond 8 entries on JVM *degrades* to hash-map automatically? `(apply array-map ...)` with >8 entries stays an array-map? In Clojure, array-map constructor always returns array-map (no promotion). Yes, `array-map` always returns PersistentArrayMap regardless of size.\n\nOn cljs: array-map → persistent array map, same. On cljd (Dart): array-map/hash-map exist? ClojureDart implements array-map and hash-map... The test must be portable — the ruling says \"available ordinary array-map/hash-map representations\" — use them but the corpus must work on all hosts. ClojureDart supports array-map/hash-map fns (they're in clojure.core). I'll use them.\n\nFloats: include 1.5, floats as keys/values.\n\n\"quoted collection keys\": `'(quote [1 2])` — a quoted form as key. When compiled standalone, `(quote [1 2])` inside a collection is NOT constant-per-constant-form? (seq? form) top-level?=false → `constant-form?` for seq = top-level? && quote — nested quote inside a collection: `(every? #(constant-form? % false) form)` for the map — entry key `'(quote [1 2])` is a seq (quote [1 2]) with top-level?=false → false → dynamic → the whole map is constructed, and the key compiles via compile-form → compile-quote → literal. Good: quoted collection keys reach the emitter as compiled literal children.\n\nNested maps/sets/vectors as VALUES with dynamic content — recursion covers.\n\nNow — TEST FAMILY 5's \"no duplication\" and \"set-value collisions\": `(let [x 2] #{x 2})` → constructed set with elements [x, 2] → evaluates to #{2}. Element occurrences preserved pre-runtime: assert the compiled constructor has 2 element operands + seed even though both evaluate to 2 (runtime dedup is the set's job).\n\n\"Explicit assoc/conj calls, dynamic vectors, let, and do MUST retain written order; swapping their effectful arguments MUST remain observable and their ordered canonical rows MUST remain distinct\": compile `(assoc {} (tap :k1) (tap :v1) (tap :k2) (tap :v2))` vs swapped → different roots, and runtime trace follows written order. Dynamic vectors `[a b]` keep order (constructor vector branch). let/do order.\n\nNow — about the trace primitive on 4 lanes: I'll register a host module 'ord with op 'tap. Host module registration: `module/register-host-module registry name fns profiles`. Variable name to call: `ord/tap`? clojure_test's probe/note pattern: `(host 'probe [['note fn profile]])` then variables named `probe/note`. But those run under the convention lane (native + modules). For NATIVE compile (no convention), `(ord/tap :k1)` compiles as an ordinary application of variable `ord/tap` — resolved via module registry at runtime. The walkers resolve namespaced symbols through modules? In clojure_test, run-profile-outcome rewrites to `probe/note` variable in an outcome program (convention). For native... let me check how module-qualified variables resolve in the walker — get-variable: primitives/modules lookup by full symbol. I think `module/resolve` handles 'mod/op names. The stream tests: `(dao.stream.apply/call ...)` compiles to a dedicated node, not variable. Hmm.\n\nLet me find a native test calling a module-qualified fn... `cell/get` appears in unit-opts programs (u/sexp->uast '(fn ... (cell/get ...))). And clojure_test line 2239+: `{:bridge {:op/tick #(swap! n inc)}}` — a BRIDGE option, not module. Hmm, what's :bridge? Let me look at that test (stream ops). Actually simpler: avoid module-qualified names — bind the tap function in the ENVIRONMENT: `(tu/create-vm (merge opts {:env {'tap (fn [x] ...)}}))`. Do host fns in env work across the four runners? The walkers look up free variables in env. Host function values — callable? `vm/primitives` values are host fns and get called, so host fn application is supported generally. If env-supplied fns are treated as ordinary values, application of a non-fn would error... Let me check how application dispatches on host fns — search \"fn?\" in semantic/ast_walker.","signature":"4110364c75054786875083ed"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"f3b35c91-cea5-4fe4-ac40-a98a4a623e84","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:26:16.618Z","thinking_duration_ms":56624,"thinking_display":"updates"}
