{}` literal root — stable across all constructors.\n\nNote: the anon map's `:yin.k/source` VALUE root differs between the no-source variant (59f93b7e = {:line 46 :column 39}) and with-source (fb4a46fd = {:line 7 :column 2}). Both fine.\n\nIMPORTANT design consequence: since reader metadata attaches positions to forms from TEST SOURCE files, my permutation tests must construct forms in ways with CONTROLLED metadata — permutations built at runtime via `apply array-map` produce fresh maps (the map itself has no meta; keys are quoted literals from my file → carry file positions, but PERMUTATIONS of the same entry objects keep each entry's metadata identical — so cross-permutation comparison is fine: same objects, same metadata, only insertion order differs. The compiled AST nodes' metadata merge from the form — same per entry regardless of position. Good.\n\nBUT careful for family 4's \"changing only source-position metadata MUST NOT change code order/root\": compare form without meta vs with-meta positions — root must be equal. Since compile-form-1 merges meta onto the AST NODE (vary-meta) and projection drops node meta — root unchanged. ✓.\n\nNow — the pair-sorted? oracle function in my diag had a bug risk: `(partition 2 (rest roots))` pairs must be [k v]; comparing consecutive pair VECTORS via canonical-compare — my diag code: `(partition 2 1 (partition 2 (rest roots)))` gives pairs of pair-vectors; `(vec a)` — partition gives lists, I vec'd them. Result `false` = not sorted pre-fix. \n\nAlso important: does `jing/canonical-compare` handle vectors of KEYWORDS? Yes — canonical CBOR of vectors of keywords.\n\nNow, an important question about **Jing canonical CBOR and the \":segment/blake3-…\" keyword vector comparison\": keyword with namespace — CBOR text encoding \"segment/blake3-201f…\"? The compare is bytewise on the encoded form — deterministic. Fine.\n\nNext: what about **`arity-error-form` as covered producer**: the ruling says arity-error-form is covered, not a second authority — no edit needed there. ✓.\n\nNow let me consider **how `sort-by` handles the comparator across hosts** and write the implementation. Also double-check ClojureDart's `sort-by` availability. clojure.core/sort-by exists in ClojureDart. Its stability: irrelevant for content (ties identical). BUT — one more consideration: ClojureDart sort comparator protocol expects negative/zero/positive — canonical-compare returns exactly that. ✓.\n\nAlso `sort-by` returns a lazy? In Clojure `sort-by` returns a sorted seq (not lazy). `vec` wrap.\n\nOne performance consideration: `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` per child — each call re-projects the whole subtree. For a map with N children each of depth d — fine.\n\nEDGE CASES to consider in the emitter rewrite:\n\n1. **`compile-form` recursion for children** — keep exactly `(compile-form % false env macro-env)`.\n\n2. **Sets**: `(vec form)` — set iteration order is host-dependent; sort by element root: single-element sort key `[root]`.\n\n3. **Vectors**: untouched — `(vec form)` order preserved.\n\n4. **Empty maps/sets**: `constant-form?` returns true for empty (every? on empty = true) → literal path, never reaches the emitter. Non-issue but seed still emitted for non-empty.\n\n5. **Metadata on the FORM itself**: old code — `compile-form-1` merges form meta onto the returned application node AFTER compile-form* returns. My rewrite returns the call map; compile-form-1 still merges. ✓ unchanged behavior.\n\n6. **compute each child once**: I compile each key/value once. The sort must not recompile — I keep compiled ASTs. ✓.\n\n7. **The seed**: unchanged `{:type :literal, :value seed}` first. ✓.\n\n8. **`tail?`**: unchanged via cond->. ✓.\n\n9. **What if a child AST projection throws \"Unknown AST node type\"?** Children are compile-form products — all projectable (validate-ast-call-sites! already projects final trees). BUT — wait: is that true for EVERY child? The final tree gets annotated and validated by C1 validation... is