{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026111900312370e068f460a24229","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `:cell-ref` matches in clojure.cljc:1362 are `(u/lit :cell-ref)` — a literal whose VALUE is the keyword :cell-ref — not a node type. And the others are inside quoted sexp data. So all emitted NODE types are :application/:dao.stream.apply/call/:if/:lambda/:literal/:variable — all projectable.\n\nWait, the grep counted `:type :cell-ref` twice though... line 1347 is a comment; the `:type :cell-ref` grep hits at 2307/2332 are comments/data inside quoted forms? Line 2331 `(dsg__get dsg__candidate :type)` — the grep pattern `:type :cell-ref` matched line 2332? That's `:cell-ref)` in `(dsg__equal (dsg__get dsg__candidate :type) :cell-ref)`. OK — comments/data only. Safe.\n\nNow — what about `u/sexp->uast`-generated maps INSIDE compile-program's convention preamble (the `dsg__` forms)? Those are quoted sexps compiled via `u/sexp->uast` — which \"treats its non-list collection values as literals\" (ruling RULING 2) — not via my emitter. Fine, untouched.\n\nCRITICAL next question: where else does the argument-vector path build HOST MAPS that reach compile-form? E.g. `compile-av-fn` descriptor maps — `{:yin.k/arities ...}` as constructed maps? From the diagnostics, the arity maps are the visible multi-pair cases. Others (yang.call maps) come through `u/sexp->uast` (literals — untouched).\n\nNow — design the **implementation**. Rewrite `compile-constructed-collection` + add private helper `content-root`:\n\n```clojure\n(defn- compiled-root\n  \"The canonical semantic-bytecode root of one compiled child: the content\n   address of its projected executable content, the sort key constructed\n   collections order their operands by (constructed collections v1).\"\n  [ast]\n  (:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))\n\n\n(defn- compile-constructed-collection\n  \"Lower a collection literal with dynamic elements to