fn [k v]\n       (let [ck (compile-child k), cv (compile-child v)]\n         {:sort-key [(compiled-root ck) (compiled-root cv)], :asts [ck cv]}))\n     (keys form) (vals form))\n(sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare)\n(mapcat :asts)\nvec\n```\n\nFor sets:\n```clojure\n(map (fn [e]\n       (let [ce (compile-child e)]\n         {:sort-key [(compiled-root ce)], :asts [ce]}))\n     form)\n(sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare)\n(mapcat :asts)\nvec\n```\n\nThen:\n```clojure\ncall {:type :application,\n      :operator {:type :variable, :name op},\n      :operands (into [{:type :literal, :value seed}] children)}\n```\n\nWait — for the vector branch old code did `(map #(compile-form % false env macro-env) args)` where args=(vec form) — same.\n\nNote on `(map f (keys form) (vals form))` — multiple-collection map stops at shortest; keys/vals same length. ✓ And this compiles keys+values ONCE each. ✓\n\nThe comparator: `(sort-by :sort-key jing/canonical-compare records)` — sort-by passes `(comparator (keyfn a) (keyfn b))` → canonical-compare on the two key VECTORS. Ruling: \"The concrete key is the vector of two address values\". ✓ Set: 1-element vector — fine (\"each compiled element's canonical UAST root as the sort key\" — a 1-vector wrapping is an implementation detail; canonical-compare over [r] vs [r'] equals compare of r vs r' since CBOR array-of-1 prefixes are identical). Good.\n\nOne subtlety — `sort-by` on CLJS with a keyword keyfn: fine.\n\nAlso `mapcat :asts` — transducer-less; fine.\n\nDocstring: bounds say \"precise docstrings\" allowed. Good.\n\nNow — is there any OTHER place that emits constructed collections from HOST traversal? RULING 2 scope: \"all dynamic/unquoted map and set forms reaching compile-constructed-collection\". Only the one emitter. ✓\n\n**Manifest** (frontends.cljc): revision \"c1-2\", add `:yang.frontend/lowering-profile :yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`. Keep support-profile :yang.clojure/c1. Docstring updated.\n\nNow the TEST