{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_f5800503ac014b7a856a488f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns yang.python.antlr.int-contract-fixtures\n2\t  \"The frozen exact-integer contract of Python phase C3 (slice S0 of\n3\t   docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.4), loaded on the JVM, Node, and Dart.\n4\t\n5\t   test/resources/yang/python/int-contract-v1.txt pins, for a fixed list\n6\t   of values around 2^53, 2^63 and 2^64 (and a few far ones): CPython\n7\t   3.9.6's `hash`, `str` and hex text, the guest dict-key text, the\n8\t   canonical CBOR bytes, and the `integer` module's outcomes under two\n9\t   named profiles, plus boundary operation rows. A one-off CPython script\n10\t   outside the repository wrote it; `render` computes the same text on\n11\t   this host from the integer module and Jing, so the file is a target\n12\t   later slices reproduce, never a copy of what they produce.\n13\t\n14\t   Value names are a small expression language, `[-]B[^K][(+|-)D]` with\n15\t   ordinary precedence, so `-2^63-1` is -(2^63) - 1; `f:2^K` is the\n16\t   binary64 2^K. Nothing here writes a file.\"\n17\t  #?@(:cljd [(:require [clojure.string :as str]\n18\t                       [dao.jing :as jing]\n19\t                       [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n20\t                       [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as cfx]\n21\t                       [yin.vm.integer :as integer])\n22\t             (:import [\"dart:core\" BigInt])]\n23\t      :default [(:require [clojure.string :as str]\n24\t                          [dao.jing :as jing]\n25\t                          [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n26\t                          [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as cfx]\n27\t                          [yin.vm.integer :as integer])]))\n28\t\n29\t\n30\t(def path\n31\t  \"The fixture, relative to the repository root.\"\n32\t  \"test/resources/yang/python/int-contract-v1.txt\")\n33\t\n34\t\n35\t(def generator\n36\t  \"The interpreter that computed the CPython columns.\"\n37\t  \"CPython 3.9.6\")\n38\t\n39\t\n40\t(def profiles\n41\t  \"`[name max-bits max-digits]` of the two named limit profiles: `wide`,\n42\t   the C3 contract profile (the parity runners install a wider 100000\n43\t   bits), and `small`, as the S3a limit tests run.\"\n44\t  [[\"wide\" 4096 4300]\n45\t   [\"small\" 60 5]])\n46\t\n47\t\n48\t(def int-names\n49\t  \"The integer values, boundary neighbors included.\"\n50\t  [\"0\" \"1\" \"-1\" \"2^31-1\" \"2^31\" \"2^31+1\" \"2^32\"\n51\t   \"2^53-1\" \"2^53\" \"2^53+1\" \"-2^53+1\" \"-2^53\" \"-2^53-1\"\n52\t   \"2^62\" \"2^63-1\" \"2^63\" \"2^63+1\" \"-2^63+1\" \"-2^63\" \"-2^63-1\"\n53\t   \"2^64-1\" \"2^64\" \"2^64+1\" \"-2^64+1\" \"-2^64\" \"-2^64-1\"\n54\t   \"2^100\" \"10^30\" \"2^1074\" \"2^1075\" \"2^2000\"])\n55\t\n56\t\n57\t(def float-names\n58\t  \"The integer-valued binary64 values.\"\n59\t  [\"f:2^53\" \"f:2^63\"])\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"9bd12f1e-63ef-4741-9770-08f246e70be3","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:32:09.141Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_fixtures.cljc","content":"(ns yang.python.antlr.int-contract-fixtures\n  \"The frozen exact-integer contract of Python phase C3 (slice S0 of\n   docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.4), loaded on the JVM, Node, and Dart.\n\n   test/resources/yang/python/int-contract-v1.txt pins, for a fixed list\n   of values around 2^53, 2^63 and 2^64 (and a few far ones): CPython\n   3.9.6's `hash`, `str` and hex text, the guest dict-key text, the\n   canonical CBOR bytes, and the `integer` module's outcomes under two\n   named profiles, plus boundary operation rows. A one-off CPython script\n   outside the repository wrote it; `render` computes the same text on\n   this host from the integer module and Jing, so the file is a target\n   later slices reproduce, never a copy of what they produce.\n\n   Value names are a small expression language, `[-]B[^K][(+|-)D]` with\n   ordinary precedence, so `-2^63-1` is -(2^63) - 1; `f:2^K` is the\n   binary64 2^K. Nothing here writes a file.\"\n  #?@(:cljd [(:require [clojure.string :as str]\n                       [dao.jing :as jing]\n                       [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n                       [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as cfx]\n                       [yin.vm.integer :as integer])\n             (:import [\"dart:core\" BigInt])]\n      :default [(:require [clojure.string :as str]\n                          [dao.jing :as jing]\n                          [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n                          [dao.jing.cbor-fixtures :as cfx]\n                          [yin.vm.integer :as integer])]))\n\n\n(def path\n  \"The fixture, relative to the repository root.\"\n  \"test/resources/yang/python/int-contract-v1.txt\")\n\n\n(def generator\n  \"The interpreter that computed the CPython columns.\"\n  \"CPython 3.9.6\")\n\n\n(def profiles\n  \"`[name max-bits max-digits]` of the two named limit profiles: `wide`,\n   the C3 contract profile (the parity runners install a wider 100000\n   bits), and `small`, as the S3a limit tests run.\"\n  [[\"wide\" 4096 4300]\n   [\"small\" 60 5]])\n\n\n(def int-names\n  \"The integer values, boundary neighbors included.\"\n  [\"0\" \"1\" \"-1\" \"2^31-1\" \"2^31\" \"2^31+1\" \"2^32\"\n   \"2^53-1\" \"2^53\" \"2^53+1\" \"-2^53+1\" \"-2^53\" \"-2^53-1\"\n   \"2^62\" \"2^63-1\" \"2^63\" \"2^63+1\" \"-2^63+1\" \"-2^63\" \"-2^63-1\"\n   \"2^64-1\" \"2^64\" \"2^64+1\" \"-2^64+1\" \"-2^64\" \"-2^64-1\"\n   \"2^100\" \"10^30\" \"2^1074\" \"2^1075\" \"2^2000\"])\n\n\n(def float-names\n  \"The integer-valued binary64 values.\"\n  [\"f:2^53\" \"f:2^63\"])\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":397}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"call_f5800503ac014b7a856a488f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
