ith-open [in (clojure.java.io/input-stream path)] ...)` — also JVM-specific. It's fine: jolt acceptance is already blocked by the owner dependency; I'll note the IO dependency honestly.\n\nLet me now check what a jolt-hosted clojure looks like — actually jolt_j2_lane runs test namespaces under `-M:jolt` with jolt binary — jolt implements enough clojure to run the suite. java.nio interop won't exist. To be maximally safe I could encode the fixture bytes as BASE64 TEXT inside an EDN/CLJC data literal (a def of a base64 string constant!) — no file IO at all! decode: dao.host has base64 (dao.stream.base64-test exists). `dao.jing.cbor` + base64 decode → bytes → cbor/decode. Base64 decode portable across ALL hosts including jolt, no IO. The fixture becomes a tracked source file with a giant base64 string — acceptable (few KB).\n\nBase64 decode helper: dao.stream.base64? or dao.host? Let me check what's available: dao.stream.base64-test is in J2 list... find the codec ns.","signature":"3566f1b970ff4bbd88f5ffee"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"5f032991-3f67-43b2-a46b-cd3f90c4efb3","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:35:26.621Z","thinking_duration_ms":136309,"thinking_display":"updates"}
