+ snapshot invariance across permutations/representations.\n\n**§4 Family 4 — oracle:**\n```clojure\n(deftest order-pairing-multiplicity-oracle-test ...)\n```\n- for each corpus map compile: seed-first, even count, pair-keys sorted, pairs match independently compiled entries (multiset), metadata invariance (with-meta variants), tie preservation (identical pairs kept: `{x :v x :v}` — construct with duplicate keys? A HOST MAP CANNOT HAVE DUPLICATE KEYS — `{x :v x :v}` evaluates to {x :v} — one entry! Duplicate ENTRIES can't exist in a host map. Hmm! \"add distinct forms compiling to an identical entire pair/element to exercise ties without occurrence loss\" — for MAPS: distinct forms that compile to the same PAIR — e.g. key `(quote x)` vs `x`? compile differently. Key `x` value `(+ n 0)` vs key `x` value... identical pair means identical key code AND value code: two DIFFERENT source forms compiling to identical code: e.g. key `x` (symbol) vs key form `(quote x)`? no that's quoted data. How about values: `(+ n 0)` and... two syntactically different forms with same compile: `(+ n 0)` vs `(+ n 0)` with different metadata! `(with-meta '(+ n 0) {:line 5})` vs plain — same projected content (meta dropped), different source. That's the tie: two entries whose compiled pairs are content-identical → equal sort keys → both occurrences preserved (4 operands for 2 entries). For SETS: `#{(+ n 0) (+ n 0)}` — host set DEDUPES identical forms by value! Two IDENTICAL forms are = → one element. So ties in sets need distinct-but-equivalent forms: `#{(with-meta '(+ n 0) {:line 9}) (+ n 0)}` — distinct host values (different meta) → set keeps 2 → both compile to identical code → tie. ✓ Same trick for maps.\n\n- \"Changing only source-position or variable-node metadata MUST NOT change code order/root\": compile plain vs with-meta-positions → same root; and the operand-root vectors equal.\n- \"Preserve semantic metadata in literal payloads\": quoted-with-meta key vs without → roots DIFFER (m