{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119004407337830b0f26a416d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait — I dropped the \"dynamic keys colliding at runtime: canonically last wins\" test from this version (it was in the aborted heredoc). That's an important RULING 2/3 assertion. Let me add it as its own deftest with the quote-safe technique. The design:\n\n```clojure\n(deftest colliding-keys-canonical-winner-test\n  ;; both keys evaluate to 0 at n=1; the canonically LAST pair's value wins\n  (let [pair-a [(list '- 'n 1) :a]     ; insertion-first in source A\n        pair-b [(list '- 1 'n) :b]\n        ;; independent expectation: order the compiled pairs by the ruling's\n        ;; comparator; the winner is the last pair's value\n        compiled (map (fn [[k v]] [(yang/compile k) (yang/compile v)]) [pair-a pair-b])\n        keys' (mapv (fn [[k v]] [(root-of k) (root-of v)]) compiled)\n        winner (nth [:a :b] (last (first (sort-by ... ))))]))\n```\nCleaner: order the INDEX by canonical compare of pair keys:\n```clojure\n(let [pk (fn [[k v]] [(root-of (yang/compile k)) (root-of (yang/compile v))])\n      order (sort-by (fn [i] (pk (nth pairs i))) jing/canonical-compare [0 1])\n      winner (second (nth pairs (last order)))]\n  ...run both insertions with env n=1, assert {0 winner})\n```\n\nAlso assert winner is NEITHER fixed by insertion: with two insertions both give same winner (post-fix). Pre-fix they'd differ (red).\n\nAlso the earlier trace test \"keys precede values; canonical pair order\" asserts log = [:k1 :v1 :k2 :v2] for BOTH insertion orders — meaning canonical order is k1-pair first. Is that TRUE? pair keys: (tap :k1) root vs (tap :k2) root — literal values :k1 vs :k2 differ → different roots — which sorts first is address-arbitrary! I asserted the expected trace as [:k1 :v1 :k2 :v2] WITHOUT knowing. Must derive expected from the independent builder instead. Let me rewrite that test's expectation: build expected trace from the compiled tree:\n\n```clojure\nexpected (mapcat (fn [c] (map (fn [o] (get-in o [:operands 0 :value])) ...)) ...\n```\nThe tap application is `(tap :k1)` — its operand 0 literal value :k1. So expected trace = for each pair in canonical order, [key-tap-arg, value-tap-arg]:\n```clojure\n(let [ctor (first (map-constructors (yang/compile form)))\n      ops (rest (:operands ctor))\n      arg (fn [o] (:value (first (:operands o))))  ; tap's argument\n      expected (mapcat (fn [[k v]] [(arg k) (arg v)]) (partition 2 ops))]\n  ...)\n```\nBut wait — this derives expected from the EMITTED order (circular!). No — it derives the FLATTENING (key-before-value pairing) from the emitted vector, but the ORDER itself is what the log observes... circular for order, fine for pairing. To avoid circularity for order: compute expected order from independent pair keys (roots of operands — still the emitted operands...). True independence: compile the four tap forms standalone, sort pairs [(:k1) (:v1)] vs [(:k2) (:v2)] by their roots — THAT is independent (compiling the source forms fresh):\n\n```clojure\n(let [entries [[(list 'tap :k1) (list 'tap :v1)] [(list 'tap :k2) (list 'tap :v2)]]\n      ordered (sort-by (fn [[k v]] [(root-of (yang/compile k)) (root-of (yang/compile v))])\n                       jing/canonical-compare entries)\n      expected (into [] (mapcat (fn [[k v]] [(tap-arg k) (tap-arg v)])) ordered)]\n```\nwhere tap-arg extracts `(:operands 0 :value)` of the compiled form. GOOD — fully independent.\n\nLet me rewrite that first testing block via patch, and add the colliding-winner test. I'll patch the file.","signature":"436b658d238e414aa2fa0b93"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"e535a3d9-94fc-4d2e-8012-e00eea2ddd4b","timestamp":"2026-11-18T16:44:23.978Z","thinking_durati