{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119014102f9d64663c41e4b40","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"FOUND IT: standalone `yang/compile` compiles with `tail? = true` (the top-level default: `(compile-form form false {} ...)` wait — `(compile form)` → `(compile-form form ...)`? Let me check yang/compile: probably `(compile form tail?)` default tail? TRUE at top level. So the standalone child carries `:tail? true` (projected! `[:application :tail?]` slot) → different root. The in-tree child is tail?=false.\n\nFix the independent builders (`independent-pair-keys`, `tap-arg`-compiled forms, `pair-key` in colliding test, `entry` expectations): compile children with `tail? = false`: use `(yang/compile-form k false {})` — the public compile-form arity `(compile-form form tail? env)` exists (line 2079-2081: `([form env] ...)` `([form tail? env] ...)`) ✓. So independent builders use `(yang/compile-form k false initial-env...)` — env default {} via 2-arity `(compile-form k false)`. compile-form arities: `([form] ...) ([form env]) ([form tail? env])` — the 2-arity is (form env) NOT (form tail?)! So `(yang/compile-form k false)` would treat false as env. Use `(yang/compile-form k false {})` (3-arity) ✓.\n\nUpdate: `independent-pair-keys`, colliding test's `pair-key`, executable trace `tap-arg`/`ordered` builders, and legacy... let me find all standalone `yang/compile` uses inside expectation builders and switch to `(yang/compile-form X false {})`.\n\n- independent-pair-keys: `(yang/compile k)`/`(yang/compile v)` → compile-form 3-arity.\n- root-of usage elsewhere: `swapped-code?` compares two whole-program compiles (both top-level tail=true — consistent) ✓ fine.\n- executable \"keys precede\" block: `tap-arg` `(get-in (yang/compile form) ...)` — the tap application standalone has :tail? true but we only read the operand literal value — `get-in [:operands 0 :value]` unaffected by tail ✓ fine. But `ordered`'s sort keys use `(root-of (yang/compile k))` — BOTH keys compiled standalone with same tail?=true → ORDER consistent ✓ BUT the ORDER could differ from the emitted (tail?=false) order?? Roots differ but ORDER of [k1 v1] vs [k2 v2] — could the tail-bit flip which pair sorts first? The tail? slot belongs to the APPLICATION row — the pair key roots would both be \"standalone\" versions — their RELATIVE order: could differ from in-tree relative order! e.g. k1-standalone-root < k2-standalone-root but in-tree k2 < k1? The tail? bit is the LAST slot of the row → affects the hash arbitrarily! YES possible. So for ORDER comparisons I MUST compile with tail?=false everywhere. Fix `ordered`/`tap-arg`/pair-key in colliding + entry `pair-keys` reads... entry test reads EMITTED (fine). The oracle `independent-pair-keys` fix ✓. Colliding `pair-key` → compile-form 3-arity. Executable `ordered` sort-by keyfn → compile-form 3-arity.\n\n2. arity-map:641 — let me look after fixing these.\n3. entry-points 824, map-perm 374, set-perm 436, executable 715/720 — inspect next round.","signature":"7c0a146f531e4d309e38300b"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"5c23dc68-f715-42d2-8a4a-1dc298c6a00c","timestamp":"2026-11-18T17:41:16.576Z","thinking_duration_ms":10524,"thinking_display":"updates"}
