nical bytes equal). The COLLISION CHECK: `same-content?` prior vs row — `cbor/content=` — ALSO metadata-blind?? → no collision throw, both rows map to ONE id. So the two quoted keys mint the SAME root — a REAL TIE — different pairs ([tie-root 3] vs [tie-root 4] — wait VALUES 3 and 4 differ → pair keys differ by value root — NOT a tie!)... \n\nHmm wait — if key roots equal but value roots differ (3 vs 4), pair keys differ → order determined → permutation-invariant. So where's the tie? Root a1759695 vs bd2406ae — 2 distinct trees. Let me think about the OTHER entries: `[\"str\" 1]`, `[3 1.5]`, `['sym 2]`, `[true 5]`, `[nil 6]`, `[:kw (+ n 0)]`... the dynamic one is (+ n 0). Sorting by [key-root value-root]:\n- key roots: literals \"str\", 3, sym, (quote [1 2]), (quote [1 2]{sem}), true, nil, :kw — the two quoted keys SAME root (as analyzed) → their pairs ordered by value roots 3 vs 4 ✓ deterministic...\n\nUnless — THE METADATA IS NOT STRIPPED in one path and stripped in another, making the tie SOMETIMES: quoted-pair-key value [1 2] (no meta) vs quoted-meta-key [1 2]{:sem} — if rows differ by meta (semantic kept), roots differ → both orders fine. If equal → tie broken by value → fine. EITHER WAY deterministic!! So the divergence must be elsewhere.\n\nOH WAIT — I bet it's the STRING \"str\" and... no. Let me think about the actual difference: root a1759695 vs bd2406ae. Maybe some permutations produce maps where a KEY COLLAPSES — host map with 8 entries — entries `['sym 2]` and... all distinct keys ✓ no collapse.\n\nHmm — `'sym` — the SYMBOL key — `(list 'quote 'sym)`? NO — my corpus entry is `['sym 2]` — the KEY is the raw symbol `sym` — a VARIABLE reference (dynamic!) — key root = variable row. Fine.\n\nWhat about `[nil 6]` and `[true 5]` — literal nil/true keys ✓.\n\nHmm, maybe the divergence isn't order at all but METADATA ATTACHMENT: the corpus entries are built ONCE (with-reader-meta from my file) — permutating entries moves forms to different positions — the FORMS